## 9. Persistence model

```sql
CREATE TABLE neurons (
    id UUID PRIMARY KEY,
    population_id UUID,
    model TEXT NOT NULL,
    parameters JSONB NOT NULL,
    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    version INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 1
);

CREATE TABLE synapses (
    id UUID PRIMARY KEY,
    source_neuron_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES neurons(id),
    target_neuron_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES neurons(id),
    weight DOUBLE PRECISION NOT NULL,
    delay_ms DOUBLE PRECISION NOT NULL CHECK (delay_ms > 0),
    plasticity_rule TEXT NOT NULL,
    minimum_weight DOUBLE PRECISION,
    maximum_weight DOUBLE PRECISION,
    learning_rate DOUBLE PRECISION CHECK (learning_rate >= 0),
    update_history_reference TEXT,
    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    version INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,
    CHECK (
        minimum_weight IS NULL
        OR maximum_weight IS NULL
        OR minimum_weight <= maximum_weight
    ),
    CHECK (
        minimum_weight IS NULL
        OR weight >= minimum_weight
    ),
    CHECK (
        maximum_weight IS NULL
        OR weight <= maximum_weight
    )
);

CREATE INDEX synapses_source_idx ON synapses(source_neuron_id);
CREATE INDEX synapses_target_idx ON synapses(target_neuron_id);
```

```sql
CREATE TABLE synaptic_updates (
    id UUID PRIMARY KEY,
    synapse_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES synapses(id),
    old_weight DOUBLE PRECISION NOT NULL,
    proposed_weight DOUBLE PRECISION NOT NULL,
    applied_weight DOUBLE PRECISION,
    rule TEXT NOT NULL,
    timing_difference_ms DOUBLE PRECISION,
    learning_rate DOUBLE PRECISION NOT NULL,
    learning_signal_id UUID,
    occurred_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL
);
```

High-volume spike records should use a specialized time-series or object-store representation rather than individual transactional rows.

## 10. Example execution sequence

```text
1. Input is encoded into a percept.
2. Related memories are retrieved.
3. Relevant representations activate neural populations.
4. The simulator emits spike events.
5. Synapses transmit activation using weight and delay.
6. The plasticity service proposes STDP updates.
7. The thought engine creates candidates from active patterns and memories.
8. Attention selects a candidate.
9. The language service formulates a grounded response.
10. The experience is stored as an episode.
11. Feedback produces a reward-modulated learning signal.
12. Validated synaptic updates are applied and audited.
```

## 11. Acceptance criteria

The first integrated release should demonstrate:

- Valid creation of neurons, populations, and synapses
- Rejection of out-of-range weights and invalid delays
- Reproducible spike simulations using a fixed seed
- STDP updates based on spike timing
- Weight clamping and homeostatic control
- Associative retrieval from partial cues
- Traceable thought selection
- Episodic memory creation after each cycle
- Feedback-linked synaptic updates
- Full replay from events, configuration, and model versions

## 12. Scope boundary

The architecture may model workspace integration, memory, selection, internal state reporting, and self-monitoring. It must not label those functions as proof of consciousness. [What_The_Workers_Saw.pdf](https://onedrive.live.com?cid=816689E597770CB7&id=816689E597770CB7!s126a1fbf0ce543c2929472174bddbab0&EntityRepresentationId=f9e2b296-1d75-48b0-a2a3-dca4afa835b1) explicitly distinguishes structural similarity to consciousness theories from confirmation that subjective experience exists. [1](https://onedrive.live.com?cid=816689E597770CB7&id=816689E597770CB7!s126a1fbf0ce543c2929472174bddbab0)

